{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n**Status: the reading is CONFIRMED by executable demonstration, but the defect is narrower\nthan the title supposes.** The pre-sign drift guard does re-resolve its own pin — however\nthat pinning is *intentional and tested*, so the code is right. What is wrong is\n`docs/plan-review-gate.md`, which documents a live-HEAD drift check that the default close\npath can no longer reach.\n\nThis ticket is therefore a **documentation correction**, not a behavioural change.\n\n## Root cause — established by measurement\n\n`close_ticket` pins the completion target **once, at close entry**\n(`src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py:505`, via `_pin_completion_ref`), resolving both\nan explicit `--ref` *and the default `HEAD`* down to a concrete SHA without fetching\n(`transition_close.py:128`, `:140`, `:142`). That pinned ref is threaded through the\nauto-resume wrapper to verification (`src/rebar/_commands/close_autoresume.py:211-213`,\n`:271`, `:275-276`).\n\nThe pre-sign guard then reads `verified-at-sha` from the manifest and, when `ref` is present\nand not the literal string `\"HEAD\"`, calls `resolve_ref(ref, ..., fetch=False)` **again**\n(`transition_close.py:297`, `:312`, `:320`). Because `ref` is by then already a full SHA,\nthat is a self-comparison and can never report drift. The only branch that reads live HEAD\nis the `ref=None` fallback (`transition_close.py:322`) — and after the entry pin, `ref` is\nnever `None`.\n\n### Executable demonstration\n\nA scratch pytest exercised both paths directly, with no LLM call and no live gate:\n\n```\nPINNED pin=154b75956ef0 head_after=c1c91c36ee4f\n       outcome={'signed': True,  'cause': 'signed'}\nLIVE   verified=154b75956ef0 head_after=c1c91c36ee4f\n       outcome={'signed': False, 'cause': 'material_drifted'}\n2 passed in 1.37s\n```\n\nSame verified SHA, same subsequent HEAD move. With the pre-resolved pin it signs; with\n`ref=None` it correctly refuses as `material_drifted`. That is the mechanism, isolated.\n\n## Why the code is correct and must not change\n\nPinning is deliberate, and there is already a committed test asserting it:\n`tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_close_symbolic_ref_pins_3e21.py` (`3 passed in 7.95s`)\nasserts that a concurrent `origin/main` move still lands **signed**, bound to the originally\nverified SHA.\n\nThat is the right semantic. An attestation should certify **the commit that was actually\nverified**, not whatever HEAD happens to be at signing time. Re-resolving to live HEAD would\neither attest a tree the verifier never saw, or spuriously refuse a close because an\nunrelated change landed during the 9-11 minute gate run. Both are worse than pinning.\n\n## The actual defect\n\n`docs/plan-review-gate.md` ends its `--ref` paragraph with:\n\n> Absent `--ref`, behavior is unchanged (verify at HEAD, drift-check against `head_sha`).\n\nBoth halves of the parenthetical are now false. Absent `--ref` the close **pins HEAD to a\nSHA at entry** and then drift-checks that SHA against itself, so it does not \"drift-check\nagainst `head_sha`\" in any meaningful sense — the guard is inert on that path.\n\nThe cost of the stale sentence is real: it tells a reader that an unpinned close is\nprotected against the base moving mid-gate, so a reader debugging a stale-looking\nattestation will look for a guard failure that structurally cannot occur.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. Correct the stale sentence in `docs/plan-review-gate.md` to state what the code does:\n   the default path pins HEAD at close entry, and the resulting attestation certifies that\n   pinned commit rather than live HEAD at signing time. Name the observable consequence —\n   `verified_at_sha` may be older than current HEAD, and that is correct, not drift.\n2. Keep the existing `--ref` explanation, which is accurate.\n3. Add a test pinning the documented default-path behaviour, so the doc and the code cannot\n   drift apart again silently. The demonstration above is already most of that test.\n\n### Rejected\n\n- **Making the default path drift-check live HEAD.** This would contradict\n  `test_close_symbolic_ref_pins_3e21.py` and would make any close racing an unrelated merge\n  fail spuriously. The pin is the feature.\n- **Deleting the `ref=None` branch as dead code.** It is reachable by direct library callers\n  that bypass `close_ticket`'s pinning, and it is the correct behaviour for them.\n- **Closing this as \"works as intended\" with no change.** The doc is materially wrong and\n  actively misleads; leaving it costs the next reader an investigation.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The sentence \"Absent `--ref`, behavior is unchanged (verify at HEAD, drift-check\n  against `head_sha`)\" no longer appears in `docs/plan-review-gate.md`, replaced by text that\n  correctly describes entry-time pinning. Verified by `grep -n 'drift-check against' docs/`\n  returning no stale match.\n- [ ] The replacement text states the user-observable consequence: a completion\n  attestation's `verified_at_sha` can legitimately be older than current HEAD.\n- [ ] A test asserts the default-path behaviour — that a close whose HEAD moves after the\n  entry pin still lands **signed**, bound to the pinned SHA — so the documented claim has a\n  code oracle. Verified by the new test passing, and by it failing if the pin is removed.\n- [ ] `test_close_symbolic_ref_pins_3e21.py` still passes unchanged, confirming the existing\n  contract was not disturbed.\n- [ ] No change to `transition_close.py` behaviour. Verified by the diff touching no\n  production logic, or by explicit justification if it must.\n\n## Testing\n\n```\ngrep -n 'drift-check against' docs/\npython -m pytest tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_close_symbolic_ref_pins_3e21.py -p no:randomly -q\n```\n\nThe first must return no stale match; the second must stay at 3 passed. The new\ndefault-path test must pass with the pin in place and fail without it, so it is a regression\noracle rather than a restatement of current behaviour."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1789925046683412164,"uuid":"251826ee-05b5-4622-8176-835ee5f6b7d3"}